\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Conditional covariance in orthonormal coordinates}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{For the explicit conditional kernel of \ref{fdip-003A}, choose
orthonormal charts #{C_X:X\to\mathbb R^I} and
#{C_Y:Y\to\mathbb R^{J_Y}} with finite index sets.
Use the joint chart #{C_E=(C_X\oplus C_Y)J^{-1}}, where #{J}
is the addition isometry. Write
#{\widehat Q=C_EQC_E^{-1}} and
#{\widehat\Sigma=C_EQ^{-1}C_E^{-1}} for precision and covariance.
Their blocks are taken in the retained-then-eliminated order;
for example,
##{\begin{aligned}
\widehat A&=C_XAC_X^{-1},\quad
\widehat B=C_XBC_Y^{-1},\\
\widehat\Sigma_{XY}&=C_X\Sigma_{XY}C_Y^{-1},\quad
\widehat T=C_YTC_Y^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
The typed inverse identities of \ref{fdip-003A} now read
##{\begin{aligned}
\widehat\Sigma_{YY}&=\widehat T^{-1}>0,\\
\widehat\Sigma_{XY}\widehat\Sigma_{YY}^{-1}
 &=-\widehat A^{-1}\widehat B,\\
\widehat\Sigma_{XX}
 -\widehat\Sigma_{XY}\widehat\Sigma_{YY}^{-1}
       \widehat\Sigma_{YX}&=\widehat A^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
For an observed coordinate #{\eta=C_Yy}, these are mean
#{-\widehat A^{-1}\widehat B\eta} and covariance
#{\widehat A^{-1}}, as in
\citet{Appendix A.2, equation (A.6), p. 200}{rasmussen2006gaussian}.}

\p{Write #{U_K=\operatorname{toLp}_2} for the wrapper from
ordinary functions on a finite index set #{K} to
#{\operatorname{EuclideanSpace}(\mathbb R,K)}, with inverse
#{\operatorname{ofLp}}. The Gaussian density and volume comparison in \ref{fdip-00JU}
identifies #{(U_{I\sqcup J_Y}C_E)_*\mu_Q} with the provider's
centered Gaussian of covariance #{\widehat\Sigma>0}.
Transporting it by #{U_{I\sqcup J_Y}^{-1}} gives the ordinary
coordinate law #{\gamma=(C_E)_*\mu_Q}. In particular,
#{\widehat\Sigma^{-1}=\widehat Q} and
#{\det\widehat\Sigma=(\det Q)^{-1}} give density
#{Z_Q^{-1}w_Q(C_E^{-1}z)}. The canonical volumes and Euclidean
#{\ell^2} wrappers are matched by the orthonormal chart and
\leanref{OrthonormalBasis.measurePreserving_repr}, followed by
\leanref{PiLp.volume_preserving_ofLp}; the reverse wrapper is
\leanref{PiLp.volume_preserving_toLp}. These maps preserve their
full coordinate volumes.}
\p{For each fixed #{y}, the same positive-definite density formula,
now at mean #{-\widehat A^{-1}\widehat B C_Yy} and covariance
#{\widehat A^{-1}}, is exactly #{k_y(C_X^{-1}\xi)}.
Here #{\det\widehat A=\det A} makes its prefactor #{c_A^{-1}}.
Consequently the provider Gaussian is
#{(U_IC_X)_*\kappa(y,\cdot)} for every #{y}. This is a specialization
of the already proved density construction, including zero-dimensional
spaces.}

\p{TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.condDistrib_multivariateGaussian}
uses finite decidable retained and conditioning index types, a
measurable source #{\Omega} with finite measure #{P}, and an actual
joint random variable with the stated Gaussian law. Its covariance
must be positive semidefinite and its conditioning covariance block
positive definite. Apply it with #{\Omega=E}, #{P=\mu_Q} and
the joint variable #{U_{I\sqcup J_Y}C_E}. Its two Euclidean
components are #{U_IC_Xp_X} and #{U_{J_Y}C_Yp_Y}; both are Borel,
the source is a probability space, and the density comparison gives
the required joint law. The two positivity hypotheses hold because
#{\widehat\Sigma>0} and #{\widehat\Sigma_{YY}=\widehat T^{-1}>0}.}
\p{The actual conditioning marginal is
#{\nu_Y=(C_Yp_Y)_*\mu_Q}. The exchanged marginal formula in \ref{fdip-003A}
identifies its pullback to #{Y} with density
#{r(y)=c_AZ_Q^{-1}e^{-h_Y(y,Ty)/2}}.
The provider's conditioning marginal is #{(U_{J_Y})_*\nu_Y}.
Its kernel equality holds almost everywhere for that marginal,
or, after #{U_{J_Y}^{-1}}, for #{\nu_Y}-almost every coordinate
#{\eta}. Its mean and covariance are the displayed arrays;
at #{U_{J_Y}\eta} the provider kernel is
#{(U_IC_X)_*\kappa(C_Y^{-1}\eta,\cdot)}.
This almost-everywhere conclusion does not select values on an
exceptional set. The independent normalization, Borel parameter
measurability and rectangle identity in \ref{fdip-003A} establish
our specified version for every #{y}. A totalized Gaussian
constructor or a Markov-kernel instance alone would not identify
that conditional distribution.}
